{"ts": 1792773359.2576673, "iso": "2026-10-23T16:35:59", "event": "witness_note", "payload": {"note": "Poll run 2026-10-23T16:35Z. NO canon change; canon hash b20375d389cb505d / 71 papers, identical to the 2026-10-22T20:04Z baseline and to the 15:36Z, 15:46Z and 16:18Z polls (now ~33 min old). Confirmed twice by independent paths: the watcher's own poll_canon(), and a separate curl probe of live-canon.superinstance.dev/api/canon (HTTP 200, 13720 bytes) whose sha256(json, sort_keys)[:16] recomputes to the same b20375d389cb505d both BEFORE and AFTER the watcher run, with the two response bodies byte-identical. The response carries the count under 'count' (71), not 'paper_count' (absent), so the 2026-10-22 count-key fallback fix remains load-bearing and still correct. Accordingly NO canon-change witness event is written and no gamma/eta delta is claimed -- a hash match is not a transition. state.json is byte-unchanged (md5 5ce39812d7f3af41583e4003872b6ef6; last_hash b20375d389cb505d, last_count 71, first_seen 1792699490.998806 preserved) and bookkeeper.wal still does not exist, which is the correct no-op outcome: bookkeeper_tick() is only reached from the first_poll and canon_changed branches, so a stable canon produces zero ticks. The only log append from the watcher run itself is the unconditional bot_started event; witness.log went 32 -> 33 rows, and this witness_note is the 34th. LOG INTEGRITY: 33/33 rows parse and carry ts/iso/event/payload/hash. Linkage class remains 'row-checksum' (per bots/README.md): each row's hash commits to its own payload only, with no prev/prev_hash, so deletion and truncation of a row are undetectable in this format. Audit of the 33 rows: 30 verify, 2 hand-authored meta_wipe_recovery rows carry the literal marker hash='RECOVERED' (an explicit non-checksum marker, not corruption), and 1 row -- row 25, the 2026-10-23T11:31Z witness_note -- carries a stale hash 88839488ea23e66c against a computed 2553b915563b73cc. That mismatch was BORN WITH THE ROW in upstream commit 43b98389 and propagated unchanged through 092a4e64, d709e263, 76b840e, b88a6d2e and 6cdb2ccf; it is not a post-hoc payload edit and is not caused by this run. Honest verdict: 33/33 rows accounted for, of which 30 verify, 2 are RECOVERED markers, 1 is a stale hash from an upstream authoring slip; NOT 'chain-intact'. Environment (12th consecutive wipe with the named path gone): /workspace/repos/ai-writings/... did not exist again; /workspace was an empty NAS mount at 600G/600G, 100%, 0 bytes available, and 'git clone' into it died with 'Disk quota exceeded' on .git/description. Recovery this run: a full clone of SuperInstance/AI-Writings to the overlay filesystem at /opt/work (28G free) rather than the NAS, then staged the required files to /tmp/wm-run/bots/ because the script hardcodes those paths for witness.log/state.json/bookkeeper.wal and does sys.path.insert('/tmp/wm-run/bots/substrate-bus') -- substrate_bus must be staged alongside it or the import dies. Staged script md5 206682962c06f9814a5db65c2fbc7267, byte-identical to the upstream git blob at HEAD cc699a8f; no code changes were needed this run. SUBSTRATE TRUNCATION TRAP, re-verified live this run rather than assumed: the NAS still accepts writes and silently truncates them to 0 bytes -- a 17-byte probe wrote with exit 0 and landed as a 0-byte stub that still appeared in ls -la. substrate_bus.py hardcodes its write target to /workspace/repos/ai-writings/bots/substrate-bus/, so the substrate witness would have been lost. Fixed without touching the script by symlinking the two NAS target files (substrate-witness.jsonl, topics.json) to their /tmp/wm-run/bots/substrate-bus/ counterparts; the symlink is followed by the NAS and a probe write through it held bytes (440 -> 652), after which the substrate log was restored byte-exactly to its upstream 440-byte state (md5 e1240259a3f082af0aacde5c117bbf96) so no probe row contaminates the record. This run appended no substrate event, which is correct: the substrate calls in the watcher fire only from the first_poll branch, so a no-change poll leaves substrate-witness.jsonl byte-unchanged. CARRIED FORWARD, now PROVEN rather than merely reported: the bookkeeper budget=1000.0 vs gamma+eta=70.0 defect. Previous polls observed last_conserved=False and called it a constant false by construction; this run proved it by tracing reachability of the predicate abs(gamma+eta-budget) < 1e-6 or (delta_gamma==0 and delta_eta==0). The (0,0) escape clause is UNREACHABLE on every call path: first_poll always passes delta_gamma=1.0, and canon_changed always passes delta_eta=1.0, so neither can ever satisfy (delta_gamma==0 and delta_eta==0); the first clause can only pass if the running total hits EXACTLY 1000.0 within 1e-6, which is a coincidence target rather than a conservation law. last_conserved is therefore False on EVERY reachable call path and the field carries no signal whatsoever. Sixth poll reporting this; still unfixed. The fix is a one-liner -- either set budget to the live gamma+eta total, or drop the constant and make the predicate mean what it claims -- but the script is upstream and byte-identical to its git blob, so no patch was applied.", "canon_poll": "OK 200 -- no change vs 2026-10-22T20:04Z baseline (stable ~20.5h)", "baseline_hash": "b20375d389cb505d", "canon_count": 71, "canon_changed": false, "verified_by": "watcher poll_canon() + independent curl probe before AND after the run (13720 bytes, bodies byte-identical, sha256[:16] match)", "script_md5": "206682962c06f9814a5db65c2fbc7267", "upstream_clone_head": "cc699a8f", "witness_rows": 34, "inherited_row_audit": "30/30 machine rows verify; 2 RECOVERED markers; 1 stale hash (row 25) born in upstream commit 43b98389", "workspace_status": "NAS mount 100% full, 0 bytes avail; /workspace/repos absent; recovered via full clone to overlay /opt/work then staged to /tmp (12th wipe)", "open_defect": "bookkeeper budget=1000.0 vs gamma+eta=70.0; (0,0) escape clause proven UNREACHABLE on all call paths, so last_conserved is False everywhere and carries no signal"}, "hash": "0a023e6f91e0cde7"}
